 ### L-block (LB)
-An L-block is a 3-dimensional iso-rectangular block of enhanced vertex information, defined by a template $(l_1, l_2, l_3)$ representing the number of vertices in the $x$, $y$, and $z$ directions [S1, unofficial]. It is used to compress the EVDS by grouping connected vertices. A single voxel is a $(1,1,1)LB$ [S1, unofficial]. L-blocks sharing an active edge are assumed to be connected and are stored in a hierarchical structure called an L-block covering (LBC) [S1, unofficial].
+An L-block is a 3-dimensional iso-rectangular block of enhanced vertex information, defined by a template $(l_1, l_2, l_3)$ representing the number of vertices in the $x$, $y$, and $z$ directions [S1, unofficial]. It is used to compress the EVDS by grouping connected vertices [S1, unofficial]. A single voxel is a $(1,1,1)LB$ [S1, unofficial]. L-blocks sharing an active edge are assumed to be connected and are stored in a hierarchical structure called an L-block covering (LBC) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Depends on the segmentation decision function. If the decision function is $O(1)$ per edge, the construction is $O(N \cdot C)$ where $N$ is the number of vertices and $C$ is the connectivity level [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N \cdot C)$ bits for edge labels plus $O(N)$ for voxel values. L-blocks add overhead for headers but reduce storage for large homogeneous regions if the LBC is used [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Provides explicit topological connectivity, enabling efficient topological analysis and hierarchical compression. Separates segmentation from visualization [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Relies heavily on the quality of the segmentation decision function. Higher connectivity levels increase storage [S1, unofficial].
